 # When Acting on Knowledge Makes Free Evidence Harmful: Good's Theorem in Normality Models
 
-**Abstract.** Knowledge-based decision theory tells an agent to maximize expected utility with probabilities conditioned on what she knows. I examine this rule inside Goodman and Salow's normality theory of inductive knowledge. In statist models it gives every free question non-negative value at a situation if and only if the agent's knowledge is *gapped*: each possibility she leaves open is sufficiently more normal than each evidential possibility she rules out. More generally, any factive state that leaves open whatever rational belief leaves open can be safely conditioned on only if it is gapped. In graded models, including Goodman and Salow's model of measurement, a state is gapped only if it coincides with the evidence; there a free second weighing has negative value at ten-to-one stakes. An expected-utility form of Salow's Conditional Knowledge Dominance does not help. It fails wherever the unconditional rule fails, and in non-statist models its verdicts turn on how conditional knowledge is read.
+**Abstract.** Knowledge-based decision theory tells an agent to maximize expected utility with probabilities conditioned on what she knows. I examine this rule inside Goodman and Salow's normality theory of inductive knowledge. In finite statist, comparable models, with K-normality and the specified questions available, it gives every free question non-negative value at a situation if and only if the agent's knowledge is *gapped*: each possibility she leaves open is sufficiently more normal than each evidential possibility she rules out. More generally, any factive state that leaves open whatever rational belief leaves open can be safely conditioned on only if it is gapped. In graded models a factive gapped state must coincide with the evidence. A separate positive-measure argument extends the negative result to their Gaussian measurement model; at normal situations a second weighing has negative value at ten-to-one stakes. An expected-utility form of Salow's Conditional Knowledge Dominance does not help. It fails wherever the unconditional rule fails, and in non-statist models its verdicts turn on how conditional knowledge is read.
 
