 ## 7. Conclusion
 
-In statist normality models, expected utility conditioned on knowledge respects "look before you leap" exactly where knowledge is gapped. Any factive state that leaves open what rational belief leaves open needs the same condition. On graded normality, which Goodman and Salow defend for measurement, that condition means not going beyond the evidence. The expected-utility form of Salow's Conditional Knowledge Dominance does not escape: it disvalues free evidence wherever the unconditional rule does, and in non-statist models its success depends on identifying conditional with post-discovery knowledge. What has not been shown is that no other way of letting all-out attitudes guide graded choice could preserve Salow's dissolution. What follows is a choice for knowledge-first decision theory: give up Good's theorem as a constraint, adopt collapse, or let probabilities conditioned on evidence set the odds and confine knowledge to qualitative roles.
+In finite statist, comparable models with K-normality and the specified questions available, expected utility conditioned on knowledge makes every free question safe exactly where knowledge is gapped. Any factive state that leaves open what rational belief leaves open needs the same condition. On graded normality, which Goodman and Salow defend for measurement, that condition means not going beyond the evidence. The expected-utility form of Salow's Conditional Knowledge Dominance does not escape: it disvalues free evidence wherever the unconditional rule does, and in non-statist models its success depends on identifying conditional with post-discovery knowledge. What has not been shown is that no other way of letting all-out attitudes guide graded choice could preserve Salow's dissolution. The result places a precise burden on this conditionalizing form of knowledge-first decision theory. It must weaken the universal safety requirement, restrict its model or question class, or change how knowledge controls numerical choice. Collapse is one sufficient restriction, and evidence-based probabilities are one alternative; the results do not establish an exhaustive trilemma for every knowledge-first theory.
 
